From: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: frontend: raise suniv DEFE mod clock to 384 MHz

The suniv DEFE needs ~6 mod-clk cycles per NV12 pixel (measured on
F1C200s by polling DEFE_LINT_CTRL[27:16], the frontend output line
counter, against the TCON line counter in TCON_DEBUG_INFO[27:16]).
At the mainline-default 300 MHz that caps the scanout at ~63 ns/px,
i.e. a ~627-pixel line per 12.7 us scanline of the boe_035 720x1280
panel (64 MHz dotclock).

Any frontend plane wider than that misses line deadlines: the
measured FE line rate at 720 px wide is 0.87 lines per TCON line, the
backend re-displays the previous line for every missed one, and the
plane appears vertically stretched by ~15% (frame timing itself is
unaffected - vblank rate and TCON FIFO status stay clean). At 512 px
wide the FE tracks 1:1 and the picture is correct, which matched the
observed good/bad split between 512- and 700/720-wide buffers.

Give the suniv frontend its own mod-clk rate of 384 MHz. On this chip
the de-fe mux offers pll-video and pll-periph (600 MHz): 384 MHz is an
exact hit on pll-video/1 while the panel is up (pll-video = 6x the
64 MHz dotclock), verified to bring the 700/720-wide FE line rate back
to 1.0. Since pll-video scales with the dotclock and the line-time
budget scales inversely with it, pll-video/1 keeps roughly the same
width headroom (~htotal x dclk_div / 6 px) for other panels too; if
pll-video sits at an unusable rate when the frontend resumes, the
rounding falls back to pll-periph/2 = 300 MHz, the old behaviour.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_frontend.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_frontend.c
@@ -686,7 +686,7 @@
 	struct sun4i_frontend *frontend = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	int ret;
 
-	clk_set_rate(frontend->mod_clk, 300000000);
+	clk_set_rate(frontend->mod_clk, frontend->data->mod_rate);
 
 	clk_prepare_enable(frontend->bus_clk);
 	clk_prepare_enable(frontend->mod_clk);
@@ -732,17 +732,29 @@
 static const struct sun4i_frontend_data sun4i_a10_frontend = {
 	.ch_phase		= { 0x000, 0xfc000 },
 	.has_coef_rdy		= true,
+	.mod_rate		= 300000000,
 };
 
 static const struct sun4i_frontend_data sun8i_a33_frontend = {
 	.ch_phase		= { 0x400, 0xfc400 },
 	.has_coef_access_ctrl	= true,
+	.mod_rate		= 300000000,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The suniv DEFE needs ~6 clock cycles per NV12 pixel, so at the usual
+ * 300 MHz a line wider than ~630 pixels can no longer be produced within
+ * one scanline of the 720x1280 panels (12.7us): the backend then re-shows
+ * the previous line and the plane appears vertically stretched. Ask for
+ * 384 MHz instead: on this chip that is an exact hit on pll-video/1
+ * (whose rate scales with the pixel clock, so the line-time budget scales
+ * along), with pll-periph/2 = 300 MHz as the fallback pick.
+ */
 static const struct sun4i_frontend_data suniv_f1c100s_frontend = {
 	.ch_phase		= { 0x000, 0xfc000 },
 	.has_coef_rdy		= true,
 	.has_coef_access_ctrl	= true,
+	.mod_rate		= 384000000,
 };
 
 const struct of_device_id sun4i_frontend_of_table[] = {
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_frontend.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_frontend.h
@@ -120,6 +120,7 @@
 	bool	has_coef_access_ctrl;
 	bool	has_coef_rdy;
 	u32	ch_phase[2];
+	unsigned long	mod_rate;
 };
 
 struct sun4i_frontend {
